# JMAP autodiscovery (RFC 8620 §2.2).
#
# A client that knows only an email address resolves the server in two steps:
# the _jmap._tcp SRV record, then — failing that — this well-known path on the
# domain itself. Both were broken until 2026-08-13:
#
#   SRV        Cloudflare rewrites the target of any SRV pointing at a PROXIED
#              hostname into `_dc-srv.<hash>._jmap._tcp.<domain>`. That name
#              resolves, but no TLS certificate can cover a label containing
#              underscores, so the handshake fails outright. app.bullmoose.cc is
#              a proxied Pages domain, so no working SRV can be served for it —
#              the record is removed rather than left as a trap, because a
#              client that finds a SRV record and cannot connect may error
#              instead of falling back to this path.
#
#   well-known The apex is this marketing site, whose catch-all answered
#              /.well-known/jmap with 200 and the homepage HTML. A success
#              status carrying a webpage is worse than a 404: it tells the
#              client it found the server.
#
# So the apex now hands discovery to the app origin, which serves the real
# session resource (401 JSON without credentials — an honest answer).
/.well-known/jmap  https://app.bullmoose.cc/.well-known/jmap  302
